  "story": {
    "bet": [
      {
        "text": "A swerve so fast that the robot has to be light, small and simple; everything on it is light and simple, which the team says is great for reliability.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s6"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "demand": [
      {
        "text": "From scoring data: a 22 point auto, swerve for the quickest cycles, an accurate shooter and a climber that can reach traversal. The upper hub, holding 2 cargo and shooting from the tarmac were needs; the lower hub and traversal were wants.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "In the Texas finals the plan was to outshoot the opponents and climb.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s19"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "auto": [
      {
        "text": "The design goal was a 22 point auto. The robot follows smooth splines with a velocity and acceleration set for every moment.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s4"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "cycle": [
      {
        "text": "The intake stays on. Cargo touches moving parts all the way up; the feeder stops at the first ball and the intake at the second. The turret always tracks the hub, so the driver just presses shoot.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "The favored ring is from the tarmac to the launch pad.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s6"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "endgame": [
      {
        "text": "Extend, grab the mid rung and pull down. At a software set point the center arm reaches the high rung, pulls down until the static hooks ratchet on, and does it again for traversal.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "At the Texas Championship, at full power with software automation, about seven seconds from the ground.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s6"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "gave_up": [
      {
        "text": "The intake cannot retract, which cost some minor penalties at district events. The L4 drive ratio was taken when L3 was out of stock. A second Limelight, to show drivers cargo behind the hub, was not used at the championship.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s16",
          "s13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "After winning the Texas Championship the drive team said the playoffs were a little rough, with some reliability issues.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s19"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "changed": [
      {
        "text": "The March document lists a NEO 550 intake at 2:1, a NEO indexer and 13 degrees of hood; the Texas Championship interview gives a Falcon intake at 4:3 after five or six versions, a Falcon indexer and about 24 degrees.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s4",
          "s6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "The climb was under manual control in mid March and automated by the Texas Championship. Black tread was added where cargo enters, because compression varied.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s8",
          "s6"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "outcome": [
      {
        "text": "62-12-0 in official play; ranked 91 by championship OPR (44.41, Roebling).",
        "basis": "record",
        "src": [
          "s1",
          "s2"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
